# NestJS patterns

Idiomatic module/provider/request-pipeline design for NestJS, and the
anti-patterns that recur in Nest codebases specifically. Applies only to
`*.ts` files in a NestJS project; general TypeScript design guidance still
comes from `ts-js-node`'s own rules.

## Module and provider design

- A feature module owns its controllers, services, and the providers those
  services need; it imports other feature modules only for the providers
  it actually consumes, and exports only what other modules are meant to
  inject.
- Break a real circular dependency between two modules with `forwardRef()`
  on both sides of the `imports`/`providers` reference — but treat
  `forwardRef()` as a signal to reconsider the boundary first: a genuine
  circular need between two feature modules is often better modeled as a
  shared third module both depend on.
- Prefer constructor injection over property injection (`@Inject()` on a
  class field) except where a truly optional dependency needs
  `@Optional()` — constructor injection makes required dependencies
  visible in the type signature and fails fast at module-compile time.
- Default provider scope (`Default`/Singleton) is correct for stateless
  services. Reach for `Scope.REQUEST` only when the provider genuinely
  needs per-request state (e.g. the current user from a request-scoped
  `REQUEST` token); a `REQUEST`-scoped provider re-instantiates its entire
  injection subtree per request, which has a real performance cost — do
  not default to it "to be safe."

## Guards, interceptors, pipes, and filters

- Cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, response shaping, validation)
  belong in a guard/interceptor/pipe/filter, not duplicated inline in every
  controller method that needs it.
- Register a cross-cutting concern that applies to (nearly) every route as
  a global provider via `APP_GUARD`/`APP_INTERCEPTOR`/`APP_PIPE`/
  `APP_FILTER` in a module's `providers` array (this keeps it inside Nest's
  DI graph, unlike `app.useGlobalGuards()` called on the bootstrapped
  instance), not by decorating every controller by hand.
- When a global guard protects most routes, mark the exceptions explicitly
  with a custom decorator read via `Reflector` (e.g. `@Public()`) rather
  than leaving some controllers undecorated and hoping the guard's default
  is safe — an undecorated route should never be silently open just
  because the guard's fallback happens to allow it.
- A guard, interceptor, or pipe class stays free of business logic: a guard
  decides allow/deny, an interceptor transforms/observes the
  request-response stream, a pipe transforms/validates one argument. Move
  any decision that needs several service calls into the service layer and
  have the guard call that service.

## Controllers vs. services

- A controller method parses/validates input (via DTO + `ValidationPipe`),
  delegates to exactly one service call for the actual work, and shapes
  the HTTP response — it does not call a repository/ORM directly, does not
  branch on business rules, and does not orchestrate multiple service
  calls that a service method could compose instead.
- A service method has an explicit return type and throws a Nest HTTP
  exception (see `rules/coding-style.mdc`) or a domain error the caller
  translates — it never imports `Request`/`Response` from the HTTP
  framework except for a documented streaming/file-download case.

## Anti-patterns to avoid

- Injecting the whole `ModuleRef` or reaching for
  `moduleRef.get(Provider, { strict: false })` to route around a missing
  `imports`/`exports` entry — fix the module graph instead; a lazy
  `ModuleRef.get` lookup hides a real DI wiring mistake.
- A provider registered with `useValue`/`useFactory` whose factory has side
  effects beyond constructing the value (e.g. opening a connection with no
  corresponding `OnModuleDestroy` cleanup) — pair any resource-acquiring
  factory provider with the matching lifecycle hook.
- Business rules encoded only as `@Roles()`/`@Permissions()` decorator
  strings scattered per-endpoint with no single source of truth for what
  each role can do — centralize the role/permission matrix so a guard
  reads it instead of re-deriving it per route.
